        "Erik H. M. Heijne",
        "Josef Kemmer",
        "Sherwood I. Parker"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Silicon microstrip and hybrid-pixel detectors fundamentally changed precision tracking and imaging, enabling major discoveries and spreading across science and medicine.",
        "attribution": "Heijne, Kemmer and Parker each have credible foundational roles in detector concepts and practical technologies.",
        "maturity": "The technologies are long-established, extensively validated, and central to modern experiments.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundary between distinct detector inventions and the many subsequent engineering contributions needs precise attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B033",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks achieved a step change in timekeeping and precision measurement, with deep consequences for metrology and fundamental tests.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye are the clear central figures in invention and development of the optical-lattice-clock approach.",
        "maturity": "Independent laboratories now achieve exceptional performance and the platform is technically and scientifically mature.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Its eventual role in redefining or disseminating the SI second remains a timing question rather than a doubt about the physics."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
